.TH OB-BASTION-SETUP 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-bastion-setup, ob-backend-setup, ob-standalone-setup \- Configure a server as an Open Bastion node with LemonLDAP::NG
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-bastion-setup
.BI \-\-portal " URL"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B ob-backend-setup
.BI \-\-portal " URL"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B ob-standalone-setup
.BI \-\-portal " URL"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
One command configures the three Open Bastion node roles.
.B ob-backend-setup
and
.B ob-standalone-setup
are symbolic links to
.BR ob-bastion-setup :
the name the command is invoked under chooses the default role, and
.B \-\-node\-role
overrides it. Every role-specific step follows the role in effect once all
options are read, so
.B "ob-bastion-setup \-\-node\-role backend"
configures exactly what
.B ob-backend-setup
does.
.TP
.B bastion
(default of
.BR ob-bastion-setup )
The SSH entry point. Accepts SSH certificates signed by the LemonLDAP::NG CA,
records every session through
.BR ob-session-recorder (8)
(sshd
.BR ForceCommand ),
allows agent forwarding, and enables the
.B ob-cert.socket
through which
.BR ob-ssh (1)
obtains the hop certificate for a backend.
.TP
.B standalone
(default of
.BR ob-standalone-setup )
A host that users log in to directly, with no backend behind it. It gets the
bastion stack unchanged; only the role recorded in
.I openbastion.conf
and reported by
.BR ob-heartbeat (8)
differs.
.TP
.B backend
(default of
.BR ob-backend-setup )
A server reached through a bastion. Accepts only certificates vouched by a
bastion (the certificate key-id names it) and, when a list is configured,
only by the bastions in
.IR /etc/open-bastion/allowed_bastions .
Creates Unix accounts on first login from LemonLDAP::NG attributes,
authorizes sudo through LemonLDAP::NG rules, and records no session (the
bastion in front of it does). Agent forwarding is disabled.
.PP
All roles configure PAM for authorization by LemonLDAP::NG
(certificate-aware, authentication refused on the password path), the NSS
module for user and group resolution, and enroll the server with
LemonLDAP::NG unless a token is provided or already present.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Common options
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-portal " " \fIURL\fR
LemonLDAP::NG portal URL (required).
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-server\-group " " \fINAME\fR
Server group name; must match a group defined in LemonLDAP::NG. Prompted for
if omitted; required with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.BI \-\-node\-role " ROLE"
Role to configure:
.BR bastion ", " standalone " or " backend .
Defaults to the role of the command name. Written to
.I openbastion.conf
and reported to the SSO.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-token\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the server token from
.I FILE
(\fB\-\fR for standard input) instead of enrolling.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-client\-id " " \fIID\fR
OIDC client_id used for enrollment. Prompted for if omitted; required with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-client\-secret\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the OIDC client_secret from
.I FILE
(\fB\-\fR for standard input). The
.B OB_CLIENT_SECRET
environment variable is an alternative. Needed for a confidential client.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Non-interactive mode. Requires
.B \-\-token\-file
unless a server token already exists, in which case it is reused.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Skip TLS certificate verification.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-security
Mode E: SSO-signed certificates only, sudo only with a LemonLDAP::NG
temporary token, key revocation list refreshed periodically by
.BR ob-krl-refresh (8)
from
.BR ob-krl-refresh.timer ,
every 30 minutes by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-krl\-refresh\-interval " minutes"
With
.BR \-\-max\-security :
refresh the key revocation list every
.I minutes
(1 to 60) instead, through the drop-in
.IR /etc/systemd/system/ob-krl-refresh.timer.d/schedule.conf .
Without it, a new run keeps the schedule the host already has, including the
interval of a 0.6
.I /etc/cron.d/open-bastion-krl
job, which the run replaces with the timer.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-service\-keys
Write the sshd drop-in that serves service-account keys from
.I /etc/open-bastion/service-accounts.d/
(AuthorizedKeysCommand, plus ExposeAuthInfo). Off by default; a run without
it removes a drop-in it generated earlier.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-sudo\-fresh\-otp
Require a fresh LemonLDAP::NG token on every sudo for SSO users
.RB ( "Defaults:%open-bastion-sudo timestamp_timeout=0" ).
Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-hardening
Apply session containment hardening (logind KillUserProcesses, nproc limit,
at/cron allow-lists, atd masked). Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-audit\-trace
Enable the primary audit trace based on
.BR auditd (8):
installs
.IR /etc/audit/rules.d/open-bastion.rules ,
enables
.B ob-audit-rotate.timer
(daily rotation; it replaces the
.I /etc/cron.daily/open-bastion-audit-rotate
script of 0.6), loads the rules and restarts auditd.
.I /etc/audit/auditd.conf
is not modified. Requires the auditd package. Off by default.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done without making changes.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help for the role in effect and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.SS Bastion and standalone options
Refused when the role is
.BR backend .
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-session\-recorder
Do not configure the session recorder and do not force it through
.BR ForceCommand .
Recording is on by default; use this only when sessions are recorded by
other means.
.SS Backend options
Refused when the role is
.BR bastion " or " standalone .
.TP
.BI \-\-allowed\-bastions " IDS"
Comma- or space-separated list of the bastion ids (as printed by
.BR ob-bastion-id (1)
on each bastion) allowed to hop to this backend. Prompted for if omitted in
an interactive run. An empty list accepts a hop from any bastion enrolled in
the project.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-any\-bastion
Answer that prompt with "any bastion" up front. Non-interactive runs already
default to it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sudo
Do not configure sudo through LemonLDAP::NG. Refused together with
.BR \-\-max\-security ,
which always replaces the sudo PAM stack.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-create\-user
Do not create Unix accounts automatically.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The script works in three phases, so that a failed enrollment never leaves
the host locked down:
.IP 1. 4
Inert preparation, rolled back on failure: downloads the SSH CA public key
(/ssh/ca), installs the principals helper (and, on a backend, the
allowed-bastions list), writes
.IR openbastion.conf ,
and on a bastion configures the session recorder.
.IP 2. 4
Checks the portal and enrolls the server.
.IP 3. 4
Lockdown: sshd drop-in, PAM for SSH, sudo (backend: LLNG rules; Mode E: token
only), ob-ssh configuration (bastion), NSS, optional hardening and audit trace,
then restarts sshd.
.SH CHANGING A HOST'S ROLE
Running the command under the other role switches the host between the
bastion stack (bastion, standalone) and a backend. The other role's sshd
drop-in is removed (backed up), and the principals helper is replaced only at
the end of the run, right before sshd is restarted; until then either helper
denies a login made through the other role's sshd configuration. A backend
disables
.B ob-cert.socket
and
.B ob-record.socket
and removes
.IR /etc/open-bastion/ssh-proxy.conf .
A bastion leaves a backend's LLNG sudo stack
.RI ( /etc/pam.d/sudo ", " /etc/pam.d/sudo-i ", " /etc/sudoers.d/open-bastion )
in place and warns about it. On an sshd without
.IR /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d ,
where the configuration is a block appended to
.IR sshd_config ,
a switch is refused: remove the old block first.
.PP
Invoked under a name other than the three above, the command configures a
bastion unless
.B \-\-node\-role
is given, and a
.B \-\-yes
run then requires
.BR \-\-node\-role .
.SH USER CREATION
On a backend, when an authorized user does not exist locally,
.B pam_openbastion
creates the account from LemonLDAP::NG attributes during the PAM session:
username from the certificate principal, GECOS, shell and group memberships
from LemonLDAP::NG, home directory created.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/open-bastion_ca.pub
SSH CA public key downloaded from LemonLDAP::NG.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-open-bastion-bastion.conf
sshd configuration of a bastion or standalone host.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-open-bastion-backend.conf
sshd configuration of a backend. Writing either drop-in removes the other.
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/ob-ssh-principals
AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand helper (bastion or backend variant).
.TP
.I /etc/open-bastion/allowed_bastions
Backend only: bastion ids allowed to hop to this host.
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/sshd
PAM configuration for SSH.
.TP
.IR /etc/pam.d/sudo ", " /etc/pam.d/sudo-i ", " /etc/sudoers.d/open-bastion
sudo configuration (backend, or any role in Mode E).
.TP
.I /etc/open-bastion/openbastion.conf
PAM module configuration, including
.BR node_role .
.TP
.IR /etc/open-bastion/nss_openbastion.conf ", " /etc/nsswitch.conf
NSS module configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/open-bastion/session-recorder.conf
Bastion only: session recorder configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/open-bastion/sessions/
Bastion only: session recordings.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Bastion, interactive:
.RS
.nf
sudo ob-bastion-setup \-\-portal https://auth.example.com \-\-server\-group bastion
.fi
.RE
.PP
Backend accepting hops from two bastions, non-interactive, with a
pre-obtained token:
.RS
.nf
sudo ob-backend-setup \-\-portal https://auth.example.com \\
    \-\-server\-group production \-\-client\-id pam\-access \\
    \-\-allowed\-bastions bastion\-01,bastion\-02 \\
    \-\-token\-file /root/server\-token.json \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
Standalone host, dry run:
.RS
.nf
sudo ob-standalone-setup \-\-portal https://auth.example.com \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Setup completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Setup failed, or an option does not apply to the role in effect.
.SH SEE ALSO
